/-
Fmt/M1PlanKnownT.lean: tests of the strict reader on proof files of the first toy (a product test)
that another program, the reference, has made. The strict reader parses a proof file, recomputes its
hash chain, tests the proof of work and checks every Merkle opening against its root. A proof here
has 22 rounds and three trees, and field elements and positions are drawn.

Tests only (#guard), with the definitions that they need; their literals are put in by a program
from the trace that came with the files. No Mathlib.
(In this head "the programs" are the C++ implementation that comes with this Lean code, and "the
format page" is docs/format/FORMAT.md, which comes with the programs.)

The files are this library's own, not the programs' (Fmt/M1FilesT.lean says what they are), at the
sizes
  K = 4;
  stages (main code, mask code, k, h, positions) ((7, 16, 3), (7, 16, 3), 2, 0, 3),
    ((8, 8, 3), (8, 32, 3), 1, 1, 3), ((9, 8, 5), (9, 128, 5), 0, 0, 5);
  6 bits of proof of work.
The head of Fmt/M1Types.lean says what K (`Par`), a stage's entries (`Stage`) and a code's three
numbers (`Code`) denote.
The parameters' bytes are the programs' own, at their tiny parameters: so the proofs are this
library's own at their parameters.

Main names:
  `pubFile`: the reference's one public file.
  `cfg`: its configuration's digest (R4.9).
  `items`: the four further items of the parameters as the programs spell them (Fmt/M1ParOf.lean:
    `itemsOf`; the format page, R12.2
    to R12.6; not held against the page as it ships). `itemsOf` spells four words a stage and one
    word of choices; for its sources see the head of Fmt/M1ParOf.lean.
  `spec`: the description (`specOf`, Fmt/M1Plan.lean).

Tests.
  the sizes meet `planOK` and `fits`;
  the plan's entries and kinds are the reference's, line for line;
  the configuration's digest;
  the parameters' bytes, byte for byte;
  the honest file is read, with the parameters' digest, the run's value, the chain's first value;
  for every round: the nonce, the drawn elements, the drawn positions, the chain's value after it,
    as the trace has them;
  the reader reads the honest file and exactly those bad files that only the decision's last two
    parts refuse, and refuses all files of form, work and openings.
The five parts of the decision are d1 form, d2 work, d3 openings, d4 the last block's linear check
and d5 its checks at the positions (`Dn`, the head of Fmt/M1FilesT.lean).

Notation:
  an entry of a plan: the round's name and eleven numbers (`Entry`, Fmt/Plan.lean, which tells
    each);
  the kinds' numbers (`Kind.num`, Fmt/Plan.lean): 1 `.elems`, 2 `.join`, 3 `.seeds`, 4 `.positions`,
    7 `.nothing`;
  ZERO: the zero-check;
  PSC: the sum-check;
  BAT: the batching block;
  FOLD: a fold;
  CS: the code switch;
  BASE: the last block
  (the heads of Fmt/M1Dec.lean and Fmt/M1Ref.lean).

Limits: this file does not say what the decision does: that the decision refuses the bad files which
the reader reads belongs to another file (the whole verifier on bytes: Fmt/M1Full.lean). This file
does not say anything on the programs' own files: its sources include no proof file of the programs.
The one exception: a test of this file compares the parameters' bytes and the public file
(`pubFile`) with the programs' own (Fmt/M1Theirs.lean).
The programs' own files of the first toy are held as bytes, or listed, in Fmt/M1ProgPTFiles.lean,
Fmt/M1ProgPRFiles.lean and Fmt/M1ProgPFFiles.lean; the honest proof of each set is read by the
strict reader, with the values taken from the programs' lists of the plan, the digests and the chain
read from the file or recomputed, in Fmt/M1EnginePT.lean, Fmt/M1EnginePR.lean and
Fmt/M1EnginePF.lean; and all are judged in Fmt/M1FullProg.lean, Run/PlanQSFProg.lean and files
Run/M1Engine*.lean.

Terms: lean/GLOSSARY.md.
-/
